          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which of the following premalignant oral lesions carries the highest risk of malignant transformation?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Leukoplakia</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Leukoplakia (a white patch) does carry a risk of malignant transformation, but it is relatively lower. Erythroplakia has substantially higher malignant potential, which is why early biopsy is always preferred over watchful waiting.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Erythroplakia</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Erythroplakia, a red velvety patch, carries a substantially higher risk of malignant transformation compared to leukoplakia. This is why early biopsy always takes priority over watchful waiting.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Oral candidiasis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Oral candidiasis (thrush) is a fungal infection, not a premalignant lesion. Unlike leukoplakia, candidal patches can be wiped off the mucosa.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Geographic tongue</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Geographic tongue is a benign condition with migratory patches of depapillation. It carries no malignant potential.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Review the comparison: erythroplakia (red, velvety) carries a much higher malignant potential than leukoplakia (white). Early biopsy of erythroplakia is essential."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. Remembering that a red patch is more dangerous than a white one is a key clinical distinction for early detection of oral cancer."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
