          "label": "<p>How do smoking and alcohol interact as risk factors for oral cancer?</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>How do smoking and alcohol interact as risk factors for oral cancer?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>They are independent risk factors with no interaction between them</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>While each is an independent risk factor on its own, the video explicitly teaches that their combined effect is synergistic — greater than the sum of each alone.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Their combined effect is synergistic, exceeding the sum of each alone</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Smoking and alcohol are independent risk factors, but when used together their combined carcinogenic effect is synergistic — multiplicative rather than simply additive.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Alcohol negates the carcinogenic effect of smoking</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>This is incorrect. Alcohol enhances, not negates, the carcinogenic effect. Together they produce a synergistic increase in oral cancer risk.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Smoking is a risk factor only when combined with alcohol use</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Smoking is an independent risk factor for oral cancer even without alcohol use. However, when both are present, their effects multiply synergistically.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Remember: smoking and alcohol each independently raise oral cancer risk, but together their effect is synergistic — greater than the sum of each alone."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Exactly. The synergistic interaction between smoking and alcohol is a cornerstone of oral cancer risk counselling."
                }
              ],
