          "label": "<p>A 48-year-old man is diagnosed with a T1N0M0 squamous cell carcinoma of the late</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 48-year-old man is diagnosed with a T1N0M0 squamous cell carcinoma of the lateral tongue. What is the most appropriate primary treatment?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Wide local excision</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Early-stage oral cavity cancer (stages I–II) is treated with a single modality. For most oral cavity cancers, surgery — wide local excision with adequate clear margins — is the preferred approach.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Surgery followed by adjuvant chemoradiation</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Combined modality treatment is reserved for advanced-stage disease (stages III–IV). T1N0M0 is early-stage, and a single modality — surgery alone — is appropriate.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Definitive chemoradiation alone</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Definitive chemoradiation as primary treatment is preferred for oropharyngeal cancers, particularly HPV-driven ones. For early-stage oral cavity cancer, surgery is the standard first-line modality.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Neoadjuvant chemotherapy followed by surgery</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Neoadjuvant chemotherapy is not the standard approach for early-stage oral cavity cancer. Wide local excision as a single modality is the preferred treatment for T1N0 disease.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "For early-stage oral cavity cancer (T1–T2, N0), a single modality is sufficient. Surgery — wide local excision with clear margins — is preferred. Combined modality treatment is reserved for stages III–IV."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. Recognising that early-stage oral cavity cancer needs only a single modality — surgery — is central to the stage-directed treatment algorithm."
                }
              ],
