          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A post-operative patient develops acute painful swelling of the parotid gland wi</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A post-operative patient develops acute painful swelling of the parotid gland with fever. What is the most appropriate initial management?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Fine-needle aspiration cytology of the swelling</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>FNAC is a diagnostic tool for suspected salivary tumours, not a treatment for acute parotitis. The clinical picture of a febrile post-operative patient with a hot, tender parotid points to bacterial parotitis requiring conservative management.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Superficial parotidectomy</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Surgery is not the first-line treatment for acute parotitis. Conservative measures — rehydration and antibiotics — should be initiated first. Surgical drainage is reserved only for abscess formation.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Sialolithotomy</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Sialolithotomy removes a salivary duct stone. This patient's presentation — post-operative, febrile, acutely swollen parotid — points to dehydration-related bacterial parotitis, not obstructive sialolithiasis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Rehydration, oral hygiene, sialogogues, and antibiotics</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Acute bacterial parotitis in a post-operative patient is caused by dehydration and reduced saliva flow. Management is conservative: rehydration, oral hygiene, sialogogues to stimulate saliva, and antibiotics. Surgical drainage is needed only if an abscess forms.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Acute bacterial parotitis in a dehydrated post-operative patient is managed conservatively: rehydration, oral hygiene, sialogogues (agents that stimulate saliva flow), and antibiotics. Surgery is indicated only if an abscess develops."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Exactly. Recognising that acute bacterial parotitis is managed conservatively — and that surgery is reserved for abscess formation — is a key treatment principle."
                }
              ],
